x86: CE5300 serial — real UART clock, the routed IRQ, and the XScale UART type

The CE5300's UART is clocked at 14.7456 MHz, not the 1.8432 MHz that
arch/x86/include/asm/serial.h assumes for legacy COM ports. Three things follow,
all found in Control4's GPL kernel drop (intel_kernel_patches/00178086, 00999901
and 00178030), which makes the same changes:

1. BASE_BAUD is 8x too small, so every divisor is 8x too small and the port
   transmits 8x too fast. Ask for console=ttyS0,115200 and the wire runs at
   921600. The boot log is then unreadable at the rate you asked for -- which
   is exactly what it did, for the entire bring-up, and it made every failed
   boot look like a silent hang. Control4's own comment on this:

       /* The Intel Media SOC Gen3 has a high freqency clock. */
       #define BASE_BAUD ( 14745600 / 16 )

   Note mainline already knows the right number on the PCI path --
   8250_pci's pbn_ce4100_1_115200 sets .base_baud = 921600 -- so only the
   legacy SERIAL_PORT_DFNS path is wrong.

2. The declared ISA IRQs 4 and 3 are not routed on this SoC, so the ports came
   up with "irq = 0". A port with no interrupt still transmits, which is why
   the console appeared to work, but it never takes an RX interrupt: reading
   from it blocks forever and returns nothing. Anything attached to ttyS1..3 is
   therefore invisible. Control4 declares IRQ 38 for all four.

3. These are NOT plain 16550A parts — the SoC's own UART block is an XScale/PXA
   variant. Control4's 8250 patch renames PORT_XSCALE's config to "GEN3_serial",
   and its whole point is UART_CAP_UUE: the UART Unit Enable bit (IER bit 6) that
   must be set for the block to drive its pins, plus UART_CAP_RTOIE for the
   receiver-timeout interrupt a short reply needs. A stock EA1 enumerates
   ttyS0/1/3 as GEN3_serial and ttyS2 (the 8051 PIC at 0x3E8) as a plain 8250.
   Generic autoconfig only detects "16550A", never sets UUE, and the IO-MCU on
   ttyS1 stays deaf — it transmits (the shift register empties) but the part
   never clocks its pins and never receives. Declare the three GEN3 ports as
   PORT_XSCALE with UPF_FIXED_TYPE so the driver latches uart_config[PORT_XSCALE]
   (UUE|RTOIE|FIFO) at config time instead of probing (a runtime `setserial uart
   XScale` does NOT do this — capabilities are only read from the type at
   config_port time). PORT_XSCALE / UPF_FIXED_TYPE resolve where SERIAL_PORT_DFNS
   is expanded (8250_core.c, which includes serial_core.h), so no new #include
   is needed. ttyS2 stays autoconfig, matching stock.

Scope: applied only to the EA family (board/ea-common/patches), which is the
only openHC target with this SoC.

Author: Joseph Telaak <joseph@telaak.dev>
---
--- a/arch/x86/include/asm/serial.h
+++ b/arch/x86/include/asm/serial.h
@@ -9,7 +9,7 @@
  * clock, since the 16550A is capable of handling a top speed of 1.5
  * megabits/second; but this requires a faster clock.
  */
-#define BASE_BAUD (1843200/16)
+#define BASE_BAUD (14745600/16)
 
 /* Standard COM flags (except for COM4, because of the 8514 problem) */
 #ifdef CONFIG_SERIAL_8250_DETECT_IRQ
@@ -22,9 +22,9 @@
 
 #define SERIAL_PORT_DFNS								\
 	/* UART		CLK		PORT	IRQ	FLAGS			    */	\
-	{ .uart = 0,	BASE_BAUD,	0x3F8,	4,	STD_COMX_FLAGS	}, /* ttyS0 */	\
-	{ .uart = 0,	BASE_BAUD,	0x2F8,	3,	STD_COMX_FLAGS	}, /* ttyS1 */	\
-	{ .uart = 0,	BASE_BAUD,	0x3E8,	4,	STD_COMX_FLAGS	}, /* ttyS2 */	\
-	{ .uart = 0,	BASE_BAUD,	0x2E8,	3,	STD_COM4_FLAGS	}, /* ttyS3 */
+	{ .uart = PORT_XSCALE,	BASE_BAUD,	0x3F8,	38,	STD_COMX_FLAGS | UPF_FIXED_TYPE	}, /* ttyS0 GEN3 */	\
+	{ .uart = PORT_XSCALE,	BASE_BAUD,	0x2F8,	38,	STD_COMX_FLAGS | UPF_FIXED_TYPE	}, /* ttyS1 GEN3 (IO-MCU) */	\
+	{ .uart = 0,		BASE_BAUD,	0x3E8,	38,	STD_COMX_FLAGS			}, /* ttyS2 8051 PIC, plain 8250 */	\
+	{ .uart = PORT_XSCALE,	BASE_BAUD,	0x2E8,	38,	STD_COM4_FLAGS | UPF_FIXED_TYPE	}, /* ttyS3 GEN3 */
 
 #endif /* _ASM_X86_SERIAL_H */
